# DB Patterns (`@stridetime/db`)

## Repository Class

Every entity gets a repository class with a singleton export.

```typescript
import { eq, and } from 'drizzle-orm';
import type { StrideDatabase } from '../db/types';
import type { Task } from '@stridetime/types';
import { tasksTable } from '../drizzle/schema';
import { generateId, now } from '../db/utils';

export class TaskRepository {
  // db is ALWAYS the first parameter on every method
  async findById(db: StrideDatabase, id: string): Promise<Task | null> {
    const row = await db.query.tasksTable.findFirst({
      where: and(eq(tasksTable.id, id), eq(tasksTable.deleted, false)),
    });
    return row ? toDomain(row) : null;
  }

  async findAll(db: StrideDatabase): Promise<Task[]> {
    const rows = await db.query.tasksTable.findMany({
      where: eq(tasksTable.deleted, false),
    });
    return rows.map(toDomain);
  }

  async create(db: StrideDatabase, task: Omit<Task, 'id'>): Promise<Task> {
    const id = generateId();
    await db.insert(tasksTable).values({ ...toDbInsert(task), id });
    return { ...task, id };
  }

  async update(db: StrideDatabase, id: string, updates: Partial<Task>): Promise<void> {
    await db.update(tasksTable)
      .set(toDbUpdate(updates))
      .where(and(eq(tasksTable.id, id), eq(tasksTable.deleted, false)));
  }

  async delete(db: StrideDatabase, id: string): Promise<void> {
    await db.update(tasksTable)
      .set({ deleted: true, updatedAt: now() })
      .where(eq(tasksTable.id, id));
  }
}

// Always export both class and singleton instance
export const taskRepo = new TaskRepository();
```

## Mapper Functions (private to repo file)

Three mappers per repository — never export them:

```typescript
// toDomain: DB row → domain type
// EXCLUDE createdAt, updatedAt, deleted from the domain object
function toDomain(row: typeof tasksTable.$inferSelect): Task {
  return {
    id: row.id,
    title: row.title,
    status: row.status as TaskStatus,
    // ... domain fields only, NO metadata columns
  };
}

// toDbInsert: domain → DB insert shape
// ADD createdAt, updatedAt, deleted flag
function toDbInsert(task: Omit<Task, 'id'>): Omit<typeof tasksTable.$inferInsert, 'id'> {
  const timestamp = now();
  return {
    ...task,
    createdAt: timestamp,
    updatedAt: timestamp,
    deleted: false,
  };
}

// toDbUpdate: domain partial → DB update shape
// ALWAYS set updatedAt
function toDbUpdate(updates: Partial<Task>): Partial<typeof tasksTable.$inferSelect> {
  return {
    ...updates,
    updatedAt: now(),
  };
}
```

## Schema Tables

Every table must include soft delete and timestamps:

```typescript
import { sqliteTable, text, integer, index } from 'drizzle-orm/sqlite-core';

export const tasksTable = sqliteTable('tasks', {
  id: text('id').primaryKey(),
  // ... domain columns ...
  title: text('title').notNull(),
  status: text('status').notNull().$type<TaskStatus>(),
  // metadata — always present
  createdAt: text('created_at').notNull(),
  updatedAt: text('updated_at').notNull(),
  deleted: integer('deleted', { mode: 'boolean' }).notNull().default(false),
}, table => [
  index('idx_tasks_status').on(table.status),
]);
```

**Schema rules:**
- `id`: `text`, primary key
- `createdAt` / `updatedAt`: `text` (ISO string)
- `deleted`: `integer` with `{ mode: 'boolean' }`, default `false`
- Column names: camelCase in TypeScript, snake_case in SQL string argument
- Use `.$type<EnumType>()` to link text columns to TypeScript enum types

**Soft delete exceptions — two cases where `deleted` may be omitted:**
1. Join tables (e.g. `team_members`) where the relationship is cascade-deleted
2. Current-state/upsert tables that represent the latest state of a single row per composite key (e.g. `workspace_user_status` has one row per `user_id + workspace_id` and is updated in place — there is no "deleted" state, only presence or absence of the row)

## Soft Delete Rules

**All queries must filter deleted records:**

```typescript
// CORRECT
where: and(eq(table.id, id), eq(table.deleted, false))

// WRONG — returns deleted records
where: eq(table.id, id)
```

No exceptions. If you need to fetch deleted records for admin purposes, that's a separate, explicitly named method.
